  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "title": "Guide | Portable Setup and Experiments",
      "text": "%md\n# Portable Notebook and Experiment Guide\n\n## Required setup\n\nChange only **base folder** and **group name** in Project Settings. Use an existing project directory with this structure:\n\n```text\n<base folder>/\n  data/training_data/                         # required to rerun the training/evaluation workflow\n  models/<group name>_lr_model/               # optional; loaded if present, fitted otherwise\n  models/<group name>_rf_model/               # optional; loaded if present, fitted otherwise\n  results/experiments_v1/                     # generated by this notebook\n```\n\nAll runtime paths are derived from base folder. To reuse the selected RF, place the COMPLETE `group2_w2_exp06_rf_model` directory under `models/` and use group name `group2_w2_exp06` (without `_rf_model`). The full notebook requires the original training data; the submitted model can also be loaded independently for inference on compatible evaluation data.\n\n## Running experiments\n\n1. On first use or after restarting the Spark interpreter, run from top to bottom.\n2. Existing models are reused with their saved parameters. Changing group name is sufficient to switch between existing experiments, even if their parameters differ.\n3. If models are absent, names ending in `baseline`, `exp02`, `exp03`, `exp04`, `exp05`, or `exp06` use the recorded experiment preset. Other NEW names expose optional parameter fields in the interface; no source-code editing is needed.\n4. In the same session with unchanged data, run Project Settings, LR, RF, Write Evaluation, Read Results, the result-name list, Chart Settings, and Experiment Comparison.\n5. Different names accumulate results. Matching result partitions are updated; existing models are never overwritten.\n6. Historical comparison and candidate-analysis paragraphs skip unavailable experiments instead of failing on a new project.\n7. Submit the complete selected model directory, this exported notebook, and the PDF report. No PDF has been written yet.\n\nThis version preserves the assignment filters, feature selection, and h_ug/h_fg formulas. The assembler prefers original columns; creating encoders does not mean the classifier uses one-hot vectors. Historical tables below describe the completed experiments, not a guarantee about a new dataset.\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
      
      "progress": 0,
      "config": {
        "tableHide": false,
        "editorSetting": {
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": true,
