      "title": "Completed Experiments | Findings and Next Steps",
      "text": "%md\n# Completed Experiments\n\nAll six configurations (baseline and exp02–exp06) have been evaluated with 233,185 training rows and 700,403 held-out rows. Every model was loaded from its saved directory before evaluation. All 700,403 test losses are non-null for every model.\n\n| Experiment | LR regParam | LR elasticNetParam | LR test h_fg | RF trees | RF depth | RF test h_fg |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| baseline | 0.001 | 0.001 | 0.156826 | 12 | 6 | 0.159079 |\n| exp02 | 0.01 | 0.001 | 0.157432 | 24 | 6 | 0.158179 |\n| exp03 | 0.1 | 0.001 | 0.160680 | 48 | 6 | 0.158563 |\n| exp04 | 0.01 | 0.5 | 0.160172 | 24 | 4 | 0.162532 |\n| exp05 | 0.01 | 1 | 0.163487 | 24 | 8 | 0.155490 |\n| exp06 | 0.0001 | 0.001 | 0.156800 | 48 | 10 | 0.153104 |\n\n## Interpretation\n\n- Stronger LR regularization (0.01 and 0.1) reduced held-out performance relative to the original baseline. Increasing the L1 share at regParam=0.01 did not beat baseline.\n- With RF depth fixed at 6, 24 trees improved on 12 trees; 48 trees did not improve on 24 trees in this run.\n- With 24 trees, depth 4 was worse than depth 6, while depth 8 improved the held-out log loss.\n- exp06 therefore tested weaker LR regularization (0.0001, elasticNetParam=0.001) and a deeper RF with 48 trees and depth 10. Both RF parameters change in this refinement, so any improvement cannot be attributed to depth alone.\n\nBest LR: `group2_w2_exp06_lr`, held-out h_fg=0.156800244.\n\nBest RF: `group2_w2_exp06_rf`, held-out h_fg=0.153104411.\n\nThe lowest held-out log loss is from **group2_w2_exp06_rf**. Its training h_fg is 0.143609283, and its test-minus-training gap is 0.009495128.\n\nModel directory: `<base folder>/models/group2_w2_exp06_rf_model`.\n\nThe held-out split was used to compare and choose hyperparameters, so it is not an untouched final evaluation set. The instructor's evaluation distribution differs from the training distribution; the best current score does not guarantee the best final score. Review feature-group errors and the charts before making the submission decision.\n\n\nThe deeper RF also has a larger training/test gap: exp06 RF has training h_fg=0.143609 and test h_fg=0.153104 (gap=0.009495), compared with exp05 RF's training h_fg=0.152635 and test h_fg=0.155490 (gap=0.002855). exp06 has the best current test score; exp05 is a useful lower-complexity alternative when discussing distribution-shift tradeoffs. A smaller gap alone does not establish better performance under distribution shift.\n\nOn all four inspected features (AGE, CREDIT_SCORE, INCENTIVE, MTM_LTV), exp06 RF has lower feature-group MAE than exp05 RF for both plotted classes. This supports the improvement within the current distribution, but does not establish robustness on the instructor's different evaluation distribution.\n\n## What to Do Next\n\n1. Review the Experiment Comparison table and Experiment Diagnostics.\n2. Compare the strongest candidate with baseline using test Series for MTM_LTV, CREDIT_SCORE, INCENTIVE, and AGE. Feature-group MAE measures observed-versus-predicted differences within feature percentile groups; it is not a causal effect.\n3. Write the approximately two-page PDF report describing the experiments, observations, distribution-shift tradeoffs, and final candidate.\n4. Export this notebook and submit the complete selected model directory. Keep the training-info directory for your records; it does not replace the Spark model.\n5. If further experiments are needed, use a new name such as group2_w2_exp07 and use the optional parameter fields in Project Settings. Do not change or reuse completed experiment names for new parameters.\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
      
