          {
            "type": "HTML",
            "data": "<div class=\"markdown-body\">\n<h1>Completed Experiments</h1>\n<p>All six configurations (baseline and exp02–exp06) have been evaluated with 233,185 training rows and 700,403 held-out rows. Every model was loaded from its saved directory before evaluation. All 700,403 test losses are non-null for every model.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Experiment</th><th align=\"right\">LR regParam</th><th align=\"right\">LR elasticNetParam</th><th align=\"right\">LR test h_fg</th><th align=\"right\">RF trees</th><th align=\"right\">RF depth</th><th align=\"right\">RF test h_fg</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>baseline</td><td align=\"right\">0.001</td><td align=\"right\">0.001</td><td align=\"right\">0.156826</td><td align=\"right\">12</td><td align=\"right\">6</td><td align=\"right\">0.159079</td></tr>\n<tr><td>exp02</td><td align=\"right\">0.01</td><td align=\"right\">0.001</td><td align=\"right\">0.157432</td><td align=\"right\">24</td><td align=\"right\">6</td><td align=\"right\">0.158179</td></tr>\n<tr><td>exp03</td><td align=\"right\">0.1</td><td align=\"right\">0.001</td><td align=\"right\">0.160680</td><td align=\"right\">48</td><td align=\"right\">6</td><td align=\"right\">0.158563</td></tr>\n<tr><td>exp04</td><td align=\"right\">0.01</td><td align=\"right\">0.5</td><td align=\"right\">0.160172</td><td align=\"right\">24</td><td align=\"right\">4</td><td align=\"right\">0.162532</td></tr>\n<tr><td>exp05</td><td align=\"right\">0.01</td><td align=\"right\">1</td><td align=\"right\">0.163487</td><td align=\"right\">24</td><td align=\"right\">8</td><td align=\"right\">0.155490</td></tr>\n<tr><td>exp06</td><td align=\"right\">0.0001</td><td align=\"right\">0.001</td><td align=\"right\">0.156800</td><td align=\"right\">48</td><td align=\"right\">10</td><td align=\"right\">0.153104</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Interpretation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stronger LR regularization (0.01 and 0.1) reduced held-out performance relative to the original baseline. Increasing the L1 share at regParam=0.01 did not beat baseline.</li>\n<li>With RF depth fixed at 6, 24 trees improved on 12 trees; 48 trees did not improve on 24 trees in this run.</li>\n<li>With 24 trees, depth 4 was worse than depth 6, while depth 8 improved the held-out log loss.</li>\n<li>exp06 therefore tested weaker LR regularization (0.0001, elasticNetParam=0.001) and a deeper RF with 48 trees and depth 10. Both RF parameters change in this refinement, so any improvement cannot be attributed to depth alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Best LR: <code>group2_w2_exp06_lr</code>, held-out h_fg=0.156800244.</p>\n<p>Best RF: <code>group2_w2_exp06_rf</code>, held-out h_fg=0.153104411.</p>\n<p>The lowest held-out log loss is from <strong>group2_w2_exp06_rf</strong>. Its training h_fg is 0.143609283, and its test-minus-training gap is 0.009495128.</p>\n<p>Model directory: <code>&lt;base folder&gt;/models/group2_w2_exp06_rf_model</code>.</p>\n<p>The held-out split was used to compare and choose hyperparameters, so it is not an untouched final evaluation set. The instructor&rsquo;s evaluation distribution differs from the training distribution; the best current score does not guarantee the best final score. Review feature-group errors and the charts before making the submission decision.</p>\n<p>The deeper RF also has a larger training/test gap: exp06 RF has training h_fg=0.143609 and test h_fg=0.153104 (gap=0.009495), compared with exp05 RF&rsquo;s training h_fg=0.152635 and test h_fg=0.155490 (gap=0.002855). exp06 has the best current test score; exp05 is a useful lower-complexity alternative when discussing distribution-shift tradeoffs. A smaller gap alone does not establish better performance under distribution shift.</p>\n<p>On all four inspected features (AGE, CREDIT_SCORE, INCENTIVE, MTM_LTV), exp06 RF has lower feature-group MAE than exp05 RF for both plotted classes. This supports the improvement within the current distribution, but does not establish robustness on the instructor&rsquo;s different evaluation distribution.</p>\n<h2>What to Do Next</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Review the Experiment Comparison table and Experiment Diagnostics.</li>\n<li>Compare the strongest candidate with baseline using test Series for MTM_LTV, CREDIT_SCORE, INCENTIVE, and AGE. Feature-group MAE measures observed-versus-predicted differences within feature percentile groups; it is not a causal effect.</li>\n<li>Write the approximately two-page PDF report describing the experiments, observations, distribution-shift tradeoffs, and final candidate.</li>\n<li>Export this notebook and submit the complete selected model directory. Keep the training-info directory for your records; it does not replace the Spark model.</li>\n<li>If further experiments are needed, use a new name such as group2_w2_exp07 and use the optional parameter fields in Project Settings. Do not change or reuse completed experiment names for new parameters.</li>\n</ol>\n\n</div>"
